What is it?
-----------

    This is a small utility to let your users customize their logon
    procedure. Rather than forcing them to view all the millions of
    boring tidbits that sysops seem to come up with, let them pick and
    choose from what is available (giving all us nasty sysops the
    perfect excuse to collect even more annoying little login thingies :-) ).

How do I use it?
----------------

    I have added my call to my TEXT:text.welcome.user file as follows:

    \f1\#2EXDEV:Logon/RoT_Logon\

    The RoT_Logon.config file *must* go into the DATA directory as
    well. Other than that, everything is controlled from the config
    file.

About the config file
---------------------

Please take a little while to get familiar with it...  I know it looks
overwhelming when you first look at it and, in future releases, I'll work
on a funky intuition frontend to handle all the tedium for you.

But, alas, for the moment it is functional and I'll I cared about in this
1.0 release was porting a working version from C-Net to Excelsior!.

In general, the logon editor was designed to display text files.  This was
a design flaw on my part from day 1 - one that will be remedied in the
next version, I promise :-)

So, meanwhile you need to use a work-around.  If you want to run another
C, DOS or ARexx program from the login editor, create a small text file that
calls the ipl commands to run the appropriate program.

e.g.  I want to add the Excelsior! C-Door "RoT_UseWALL" (hint hint! :) )
      to my login sequence.

      What you need to do is make a text file, call it wall.macro, which
      simply contains the line: \#3<path>/rot_usewall\
      Then, when you set up the entry in the login editor, display the
      textfile wall.macro.  (when that file is then actually displayed,
      it will execute the actual C-door)

Like I said, it's a bit round-about but quite functional (call my BBS to
see it in action -- right now I have probably about 20 different text files
and programs in my RoT_Logon config).

I know this is cryptic. I am likely the world's lousiest technical
writer :-) If you have ANY questions, feel free to drop me a line.  If
worst comes to worst, I'll backup my setup for you and send it to you so
you have a working model to pick apart :-)

